# `install.sh` is served from https://roark.ai/install.sh and piped into a shell.
# A copy committed with CRLF line endings still looks entirely correct - it opens
# fine in a browser, starts with `#!/bin/sh`, has the right length - and does not
# run at all: `sh` reads the `\r` as part of each command and dies on `set -eu`
# with `set: -: invalid option`. One contributor with `core.autocrlf=true` is all
# it takes, and the result is an installer broken for everyone.
#
# The marketing site refuses to serve a script with CRs, so this would surface as
# a red build in another repository. Pinning the line endings here is what stops
# it being committed in the first place.
#
# Scoped to packages/cli rather than the repository root on purpose: the root is
# vendor codegen territory, and a file there is liable to be overwritten. This
# directory is ours.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The build script is shell too, it just has no extension.
build text eol=lf
